    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_context` in `app/context.py`, and all 8 tests pass (4 existing plus 4 new).\n\n**How it works:**\n- It looks up the documents with `retriever.invoke(question, config={\"tags\": [TRACE_TAG]})`. This is the standard retriever call in LangChain, so it works with both `KeywordRetriever` and a vector-store retriever. The `\"kb-context\"` tag is attached to the lookup's trace run.\n- It goes through the results in the order the retriever returned them and skips any document whose `metadata[\"source\"]` has already been used. It stops once it has `MAX_SOURCES` entries.\n- Each entry is `\"[n] <source>\\n<text with surrounding whitespace removed>\"`. Entries are joined with one blank line, and the result is `\"\"` when nothing is found.\n\n**Tests:** I added `tests/test_context.py` to cover numbering and whitespace removal, skipping repeated sources, the 3-entry cap, the empty result, and the trace tag. For the tag test, I first passed `callbacks=[...]` to the retriever's constructor. In the installed langchain-core 1.6.5 the retriever has no `callbacks` field, so the argument was silently ignored and the handler never ran. The test now attaches the handler with `.with_config(callbacks=[...])`, which does reach it.\n\nA document with no `\"source\"` in its metadata would show up as `[n] None`. You didn't specify what should happen in that case, and our notes always set a source, so I left it that way."
  },
